  <h2>Why this age, and why the habit is worth it</h2>
  <p>
    Five is the sweet spot because the child can still be trained by story, and
    because they are not old enough to pretend they already know. A ten-year-old
    has learned that saying the right thing matters. A five-year-old is still
    willing to be wrong out loud, which is the condition every other thinking
    habit depends on. If you teach a child at five that being wrong is safe and
    questions are welcome, you do not need to unteach anything later.
  </p>
  <p>
    The habit is the same one our series keeps coming back to. Aristotle hands a
    tool, Lao Tzu shows that opposites lean on each other, and Plato asks what is
    underneath what you are looking at. Three thinkers, one habit: do not settle
    for the first answer. Start that habit now with the{' '}
    <a href="/books/the-adventures-of-the-curious-kid">Adventures of the Curious
    Kid</a>, or jump to the{' '}
    <a href="/books/the-curious-kid-meets-aristotle">Curious Kid Meets
    Aristotle</a> if your child is ready to use an idea the same day.
  </p>

